A National Longitudinal Research Center for Substance-Use Disorder Treatment

An Adaptive, Integrated, and Economically Evaluated Model for Determining Optimal Recovery Trajectories

Executive Summary

Substance-use disorders (SUDs) impose substantial clinical, social, and economic costs while existing treatment research remains limited by a fundamental methodological problem: the thing commonly described as "addiction treatment" is not a standardized intervention.

Treatment varies substantially between facilities in clinical philosophy, staffing, therapeutic intensity, medications, treatment duration, discharge practices, housing assistance, transportation access, employment support, and continuing care. Published research therefore frequently compares nominally similar treatment categories that are, in practice, materially different interventions delivered within materially different environments. This creates difficulty determining whether observed differences in outcome are attributable to the treatment itself, the institution delivering it, patient selection, treatment fidelity, post-treatment circumstances, or interactions among these variables.

A second limitation is that addiction treatment is generally studied as a series of disconnected interventions rather than as a continuous longitudinal process. Inpatient treatment, outpatient treatment, medication, psychotherapy, transitional housing, employment assistance, and recovery support are often evaluated independently even though their effects may depend upon timing, sequencing, interaction, and continuity.

A third limitation concerns economic evaluation. Existing studies measure economic benefits inconsistently, use different time horizons and outcome domains, and frequently emphasize healthcare or criminal-justice expenditures without comprehensively measuring productivity, housing stability, social-service utilization, quality of life, and longer-term societal effects. A 2023 systematic review identified only 12 eligible economic-evaluation studies from 729 initially screened records and found substantial methodological heterogeneity.

This proposal recommends creation of a publicly funded, purpose-built National Longitudinal Addiction Treatment Research Center whose primary purpose would be to determine the most effective and economically sustainable longitudinal treatment trajectories for SUD.

The center would provide a standardized treatment environment while experimentally varying specific treatment components and their timing. Patients would receive medically appropriate baseline care and would be randomized among ethically defensible treatment alternatives. Adaptive randomization and Sequential Multiple Assignment Randomized Trial (SMART) methodologies would allow treatment to change according to individual response.

The intervention would extend beyond inpatient treatment. A proposed treatment trajectory would include stabilization, intensive therapeutic intervention, transitional housing, employment and transportation support, outpatient psychotherapy, medication management, community reintegration, and long-term follow-up. These components would be studied individually and in combination.

The center would simultaneously function as:

a clinical treatment facility;
a longitudinal research laboratory;
a treatment-sequencing laboratory;
a behavioral and pharmacological research environment;
a recovery-housing research program;
an implementation-science laboratory; and
a health-economic research center.
The ultimate objective would not be to establish that one particular drug or therapy "works." It would be to determine whether a deliberately designed recovery trajectory can reliably produce superior long-term outcomes and, if so, which components, sequences, populations, and implementation conditions are responsible.

───

The Central Problem
SUD treatment is generally studied as though it were a collection of discrete interventions.

A person receives:

• inpatient treatment,
• medication,
• psychotherapy,
• outpatient treatment,
• recovery housing,
• employment assistance,
• or some combination thereof.

Researchers then attempt to determine whether a particular intervention improves outcomes.

This approach is useful but incomplete.

Recovery is not normally experienced as a collection of independent interventions. It is a trajectory through multiple environments and behavioral states.

A person may enter treatment with physiological dependence, disrupted sleep, impaired executive functioning, entrenched behavioral patterns, unstable housing, unemployment, financial stress, social isolation, legal problems, and an environment containing many cues associated with substance use.

Following treatment, that person may return immediately to the same environment.

Consequently, an intervention that produces substantial short-term improvement may fail to produce durable recovery if the subsequent environment strongly reinforces the previous behavioral pattern.

The proposed research program therefore treats addiction treatment as a longitudinal complex intervention rather than a single clinical procedure.

───

Problems With Current Research
2.1 "Inpatient treatment" is not a standardized intervention

One of the most important methodological problems is that facilities described by the same treatment category may provide substantially different experiences.

Relevant differences may include:

• staffing ratios;
• staff qualifications;
• psychiatric availability;
• medical services;
• medication practices;
• therapeutic philosophy;
• psychotherapy intensity;
• group structure;
• individual therapy;
• peer-support practices;
• treatment duration;
• behavioral expectations;
• physical environment;
• patient population;
• discharge planning;
• family involvement;
• transportation assistance;
• housing assistance;
• employment services;
• aftercare;
• and treatment fidelity.

Research has documented substantial variation in addiction-treatment services and outcomes across programs.

Consequently, an observational comparison between two treatment centers cannot necessarily determine whether one treatment model is superior.

It may instead be comparing two organizations.

───

2.2 Comparing different facilities introduces confounding at the institutional level

Suppose Facility A produces better outcomes than Facility B.

Possible explanations include:

• A provides better psychotherapy.
• A has more qualified staff.
• A treats a different patient population.
• A retains patients longer.
• A has better discharge planning.
• A has better housing connections.
• A has fewer disruptions.
• A has stronger therapeutic relationships.
• A provides better medication management.
• A has a different surrounding community.
• A attracts patients who are more motivated to recover.

Without standardized institutional conditions, these factors are difficult to disentangle.

Statistical adjustment cannot completely solve this problem because many relevant characteristics are difficult to measure or may not even be recognized in advance.

The result is a fundamental distinction:

Comparing treatments between standardized environments

is fundamentally different from

comparing treatment facilities.

The proposed center addresses this problem by holding the institutional environment substantially constant while deliberately varying the treatment variables under investigation.

