The Continuous-Trajectory Problem
Current treatment research frequently separates:

inpatient treatment

from

outpatient treatment

from

recovery housing

from

employment assistance

from

psychotherapy

from

medication.

The proposed model instead asks:

«What sequence of interventions and environments produces the highest probability of durable recovery?»

This permits investigation of questions that conventional trials are poorly equipped to answer.

Examples include:

• Should intensive psychotherapy begin immediately after stabilization or after a short period of recovery?
• Does a pharmacological intervention work better when paired with psychotherapy?
• Does psychotherapy produce greater benefit during particular periods of pharmacologically induced neuroplasticity or symptom reduction?
• How long should structured residential treatment continue?
• When should transitional housing begin?
• How long should transitional housing last?
• Does employment assistance improve long-term outcomes when introduced early or later?
• Which patients benefit from additional pharmacological intervention?
• What should happen when a patient fails to respond to the initial treatment?
• What treatment sequence minimizes relapse after an initial remission?
• What level of structure is optimal during community reintegration?

These are sequence questions, not simply treatment-versus-placebo questions.

SMART methodology was specifically developed to investigate such adaptive treatment strategies. In addiction research, SMART trials have already been used to examine sequential treatment decisions for cocaine-use disorder, including different strategies for initial responders and nonresponders.

The proposed center would extend this methodology from individual interventions to the entire recovery trajectory.

