Proposed Institution
4.1 Public ownership and funding

The proposed institution should initially be publicly funded and operated under a temporary or semi-permanent federal research authority, potentially associated with agencies such as NIH/NIDA, with appropriate participation from SAMHSA, HHS, HUD, Department of Labor, and other relevant agencies.

The purpose of public operation is not ideological.

It addresses an experimental requirement.

A conventional treatment provider has legitimate financial and operational incentives that may conflict with the demands of a long-term experimental program.

A publicly funded research institution could instead be explicitly designed around:

• scientific validity;
• patient welfare;
• treatment fidelity;
• longitudinal follow-up;
• transparency;
• reproducibility;
• and public benefit.

The center should be independent from insurers and treatment providers whose financial interests could influence treatment allocation or reporting.

───

The Center as a Standardized Experimental Environment
The center should establish a highly standardized baseline environment.

Variables held constant where possible would include:

• staffing ratios;
• minimum staff qualifications;
• medical coverage;
• psychiatric coverage;
• nutritional standards;
• physical environment;
• patient safety procedures;
• sleep opportunities;
• recreational opportunities;
• baseline educational programming;
• data collection;
• discharge criteria;
• assessment schedule;
• research ethics procedures;
• and minimum standards of patient autonomy and informed consent.

This does not mean every aspect of care should be identical.

Rather, the things being experimentally tested should be the things allowed to vary.

This distinction is essential.

───

Multiple Treatment Pathways
The center should not establish one supposedly optimal treatment pathway at the outset.

Doing so would merely substitute one untested clinical philosophy for another.

Instead, the institution should maintain several ethically defensible treatment pathways, each consisting of interventions supported by sufficient preliminary evidence to justify clinical investigation.

Possible intervention domains could include:

Pharmacological

Depending on the disorder and contemporary evidence:

• medications for opioid-use disorder;
• medications for alcohol-use disorder;
• medications targeting nicotine dependence;
• emerging pharmacotherapies for stimulant-use disorders;
• antidepressants or anxiolytics when clinically indicated;
• medications targeting sleep or withdrawal symptoms;
• emerging interventions such as GLP-1 receptor agonists where appropriate research evidence and safety justify investigation;
• psychedelic or dissociative-assisted approaches where legally and ethically authorized;
• placebo-controlled pharmacological trials where appropriate.

The center should never assume that an emerging medication is effective merely because it is mechanistically interesting.

For example, current evidence for ketamine in SUD is promising but still insufficient to establish its long-term efficacy and safety conclusively. A 2026 meta-analysis found a significant short-term abstinence signal but not a statistically significant benefit at 1–6 months.

That makes ketamine a particularly interesting research variable, not a predetermined component of treatment.

───

Psychotherapeutic Pathways
Possible pathways could include:

• CBT;
• motivational interviewing;
• contingency management;
• ACT;
• trauma-informed interventions;
• relapse-prevention approaches;
• family-based interventions;
• peer-support interventions;
• group therapy;
• individual psychotherapy;
• vocational counseling;
• behavioral activation;
• and combinations thereof.

The center could investigate both:

which psychotherapy works best

and

when psychotherapy works best.

The latter question may prove substantially more important.

───

Pharmacology–Psychotherapy Timing
One particularly important experimental domain would investigate whether certain medications create temporary changes in cognition, affect, learning, motivation, or behavioral flexibility that can be therapeutically exploited.

For example:

Pharmacological intervention

↓

altered psychological/biological state

↓

targeted psychotherapy

↓

behavioral practice

↓

environmental reinforcement

↓

consolidation

This is currently a hypothesis rather than an established general principle.

Recent reviews of ketamine-assisted psychotherapy describe promising outcomes but emphasize substantial variation in treatment protocols and insufficient evidence to determine the added value or optimal timing of psychotherapy.

A standardized research center would provide an unusually good environment for testing this directly.

───

Transitional Housing as a Core Treatment Component
Discharge should not be treated as the end of treatment.

A central component of the proposed model would be 3–12 months of structured transitional housing, with longer duration available according to predetermined clinical criteria.

Housing should be:

• stable;
• safe;
• substance-free where clinically appropriate;
• connected to public transportation;
• geographically accessible to employment opportunities;
• connected to outpatient healthcare;
• connected to psychotherapy;
• and integrated with vocational services.

Residents would progressively acquire independence rather than being abruptly discharged from institutional structure.

This is supported by an emerging evidence base.

A 2025 systematic review identified only five eligible comparative recovery-housing studies, but those studies generally found advantages over usual continuing care in abstinence, income, employment and some criminal-justice outcomes. Recovery housing also showed favorable cost-effectiveness, although the authors characterize the evidence base as moderate and emphasize the need for additional comparative research.

Thus, recovery housing should be treated not as an optional social service but as an experimentally measurable component of the recovery trajectory.

───

Employment and Transportation
Employment should likewise be treated as an intervention variable.

Possible interventions include:

• immediate employment placement;
• delayed employment after stabilization;
• vocational training;
• supported employment;
• education;
• apprenticeships;
• transportation assistance;
• public-transit access;
• driver's-license assistance where appropriate;
• and combinations thereof.

The research question would be whether employment is merely an outcome of recovery or can itself become part of the mechanism producing recovery.

This distinction is critical.

A stable job can provide:

• structure;
• income;
• social interaction;
• purpose;
• routine;
• independence;
• and an alternative source of reinforcement.

The same logic applies to transportation.

A person cannot reliably participate in therapy, employment, medical care, or social activities if those services are physically inaccessible.

