Proposed Treatment Architecture
A possible initial architecture would be:

Stage 1 — Intake and stabilization

Comprehensive assessment of:

• substance-use history;
• physical health;
• psychiatric symptoms;
• cognitive function;
• sleep;
• social environment;
• housing;
• employment;
• transportation;
• family/social support;
• legal circumstances;
• previous treatment;
• treatment preferences;
• and baseline functioning.

Medical withdrawal management and other clinically necessary treatment would be provided.

Stage 2 — Early intervention

Patients would enter one of several ethically defensible treatment pathways.

Variables could include:

• psychotherapy modality;
• psychotherapy intensity;
• pharmacological intervention;
• peer support;
• contingency management;
• behavioral activation;
• and other evidence-supported interventions.

Stage 3 — Response assessment

Patients would be classified according to predefined objective criteria.

Possible response measures include:

• toxicology;
• craving;
• withdrawal;
• psychiatric symptoms;
• sleep;
• functioning;
• treatment engagement;
• behavioral measures;
• and patient-reported outcomes.

Stage 4 — Adaptive treatment

Responders and nonresponders would receive different randomized options.

This would permit identification of:

optimal treatment for responders

and

optimal rescue strategies for nonresponders.

Stage 5 — Transitional housing

Patients would enter supported housing rather than returning immediately to their previous environment.

Stage 6 — Employment/community reintegration

Patients would receive structured access to:

• employment;
• education;
• transportation;
• outpatient healthcare;
• psychotherapy;
• peer support;
• and community activities.

Stage 7 — Progressive independence

External structure would gradually decrease according to predetermined criteria.

Stage 8 — Long-term follow-up

Follow-up should continue for at least:

• 3 months;
• 6 months;
• 12 months;
• 24 months;

with longer-term follow-up where feasible.

