Adaptive Research Rather Than One Giant Trial
The institution should function as a continuously learning clinical research environment.

Each study could answer one question while contributing data to the larger system.

For example:

Study A: optimal initial psychotherapy.

Study B: pharmacology × psychotherapy interaction.

Study C: timing of psychotherapy.

Study D: transitional-housing duration.

Study E: employment timing.

Study F: response-based treatment escalation.

Study G: relapse-prevention strategy.

The results would then inform subsequent trials.

This creates a research feedback loop:

«treatment → measurement → analysis → improved treatment protocol → new experiment → improved protocol.»

The center therefore becomes increasingly informative over time rather than restarting the evidence-generation process with every new clinical trial.

───

Primary Outcomes
The primary outcome should not simply be abstinence at discharge.

A proposed primary endpoint would be:

Sustained remission at 12 and 24 months

supplemented by:

• time to relapse;
• days of substance use;
• overdose;
• treatment retention;
• healthcare utilization;
• psychiatric hospitalization;
• emergency-department utilization;
• housing stability;
• employment;
• income;
• education;
• quality of life;
• criminal-justice involvement;
• and patient-reported recovery.

Different disorders may require disorder-specific primary endpoints.

───

Mechanistic Data
The center should also collect data capable of explaining why successful treatment works.

Possible measures include:

• longitudinal psychiatric assessments;
• cognitive testing;
• sleep;
• physiological measures;
• behavioral tasks;
• ecological momentary assessment;
• treatment engagement;
• social-network changes;
• environmental exposure;
• medication adherence;
• neurobiological measurements where justified;
• and patient-reported psychological changes.

The goal would be to distinguish:

symptom suppression

from

behavioral adaptation

from

environmental change

from

durable recovery.

This is particularly important for pharmacological interventions whose clinical effects emerge long after their immediate molecular actions.

