Economic Evaluation
Economic evaluation should be designed into the project from the beginning.

Current research is highly heterogeneous in:

• time horizon;
• outcome definitions;
• cost categories;
• data sources;
• valuation methods;
• and analytic perspective.

A 2023 systematic review found only 12 eligible economic-evaluation studies from an initial pool of 729 records, with substantial variation among the studies. The authors specifically identify methodological heterogeneity and incomplete measurement of broader societal benefits as important limitations.

The proposed center should therefore use a standardized economic framework.

Costs

Measure:

• inpatient treatment;
• medications;
• psychotherapy;
• staffing;
• housing;
• transportation;
• vocational services;
• outpatient care;
• emergency care;
• hospitalizations;
• social services;
• and research/administrative costs.

Benefits

Measure:

• avoided healthcare utilization;
• avoided emergency care;
• avoided hospitalization;
• reduced criminal-justice involvement;
• reduced incarceration;
• increased employment;
• increased income;
• reduced homelessness;
• reduced social-service utilization;
• reduced disability;
• reduced premature mortality;
• and quality-of-life gains.

The existing literature suggests that criminal activity and criminal-justice costs can represent some of the largest economic benefits associated with successful SUD intervention, while productivity and social-service effects are less consistently measured.

───

The Economic Question Should Be Societal, Not Merely Clinical
A treatment that costs 100,000 in subsequent societal costs may be economically preferable to a treatment costing $`10,000 that produces repeated relapse.

Therefore the center should report at least three perspectives:

Healthcare perspective

What does the intervention cost the healthcare system?

Government perspective

What does it cost public programs, and what expenditures does it prevent?

Societal perspective

What does the intervention cost or save across healthcare, criminal justice, productivity, housing, social services, families, and other measurable domains?

This distinction is essential because the entity paying for treatment may not be the entity receiving the financial benefit.

An insurer may pay for treatment while government saves money through reduced incarceration.

An employer may benefit from increased productivity while Medicaid pays for healthcare.

A family may experience enormous economic benefit that appears nowhere in an insurer's accounting.

The research center should therefore explicitly measure cost shifting between institutions.

───

Cost Per Successful Recovery
One of the most useful outputs would be:

Cost per additional sustained remission

Rather than merely reporting:

«"`$X per treatment episode."»

the study would report:

«"$X in total treatment expenditure produced one additional person in sustained remission at 12/24 months."»

This could then be compared with:

• usual treatment;
• repeated treatment episodes;
• untreated SUD;
• and alternative treatment pathways.

───

Net Societal Benefit
The ultimate economic metric could be:

Net societal benefit = avoided societal costs + productivity gains + quality-of-life gains − total intervention costs.

Sensitivity analyses should test assumptions about:

• the monetary value of productivity;
• criminal-justice costs;
• healthcare costs;
• housing;
• mortality;
• relapse;
• discount rates;
• and long-term durability.

The existing literature demonstrates that SUD economic benefits can be substantial, but also that current evaluations frequently lack standardized approaches and comprehensive societal measurement.

The proposed center would therefore make economic measurement one of its core scientific functions.

