Data Infrastructure
The resulting dataset could become one of the most valuable addiction-treatment datasets ever assembled.

Every participant would generate longitudinal information linking:

patient characteristics → treatment → timing → response → subsequent treatment → environment → long-term outcome → economic consequences.

This would permit analyses that are extremely difficult using conventional disconnected studies.

For example:

• Which patients benefit from which treatment?
• Which early responses predict durable remission?
• Which interventions work only when delivered in a particular sequence?
• Which interventions have diminishing returns?
• Which interventions prevent relapse?
• Which environmental factors predict relapse?
• How long should transitional housing last?
• When should employment begin?
• What predicts successful independent living?
• Which treatment failures are predictable?
• Which interventions are most cost-effective?
• Which patients require greater treatment intensity?
• Which patients can safely transition earlier?
• Which combinations produce synergistic effects?

