The Center Would Also Become an Implementation Laboratory
Once an optimal treatment trajectory has been identified, the question becomes:

«Can another institution reproduce it?»

This should be treated as a separate research phase.

Phase I — Discovery

One standardized center.

Phase II — Replication

Several purpose-built centers implementing the same protocol.

Phase III — External validation

Independent community treatment organizations implement the protocol.

Phase IV — Population implementation

The most effective model becomes a candidate for widespread public and private reimbursement.

This progression separates:

internal validity

from

replicability

from

generalizability.

───

Ethical Safeguards
The center must not become a place where patients are treated as experimental subjects without adequate protection.

All participants would receive a clinically acceptable standard of care.

Experimental randomization would occur only among ethically defensible alternatives.

Participants would retain:

• informed consent;
• the ability to withdraw;
• access to rescue treatment;
• independent clinical advocacy;
• independent ethics oversight;
• privacy protections;
• and access to appropriate evidence-based treatment when new evidence establishes superiority.

An independent Data and Safety Monitoring Board should have authority to terminate treatment arms showing unacceptable harm or overwhelming benefit.

The institutional research mission must never override the patient's immediate clinical needs.

───

Why a Single Center Is Necessary Initially
A central premise of the proposal is that the initial discovery phase should occur within one highly standardized institution.

This is not because other treatment facilities are inherently incapable of providing excellent care.

It is because they are heterogeneous experimental environments.

If the objective is to determine the causal effect of a treatment pathway, the first experiment should minimize uncontrolled institutional variation.

Once an effective treatment pathway has been identified, multi-center replication becomes essential.

Thus:

single center first

→ multi-center replication second

→ community implementation third.

The single-center requirement is therefore a strategy for establishing causal clarity, not an assertion that treatment must permanently be centralized.

───

Why Government Funding Is Appropriate
The proposed center generates a public good.

The information produced would benefit:

• public healthcare systems;
• private insurers;
• patients;
• families;
• employers;
• criminal-justice systems;
• treatment providers;
• researchers;
• and governments internationally.

A private provider has limited incentive to finance research whose primary benefit is the creation of publicly available knowledge.

Government funding is therefore justified for the same reason governments fund fundamental biomedical research: the social value of the knowledge exceeds the private commercial return.

The institution could eventually become partially self-sustaining through clinical reimbursement, research grants, and licensing of validated treatment protocols, but its research mission should remain publicly protected.

───

Potential Global Utility
The center would not need to become a permanent national treatment network to have global significance.

Its greatest product could be knowledge.

If it identified a treatment architecture producing substantially better outcomes, the protocol could be:

• published;
• independently replicated;
• translated;
• adapted to different healthcare systems;
• incorporated into clinical guidelines;
• used to establish reimbursement standards;
• and evaluated internationally.

Countries with different healthcare systems could then determine which components are transferable.

The resulting scientific product would therefore be analogous to a validated treatment platform rather than a single medication.

