The Most Important Conceptual Shift
The central hypothesis of this proposal is not:

«"Treatment X is better than treatment Y."»

It is:

«Recovery may be an emergent property of an appropriately timed sequence of biological, psychological, behavioral, social, and environmental interventions.»

This means that the effectiveness of an individual intervention may depend partly upon:

• what came before it;
• what follows it;
• the patient's current state;
• the patient's environment;
• and whether the patient has an opportunity to consolidate the changes produced by treatment.

Consequently, optimizing addiction treatment may require optimizing trajectories, rather than optimizing isolated interventions.

───

Expected Scientific Contribution
The center could provide several forms of knowledge that the existing treatment literature struggles to produce.

Causal knowledge

Which interventions actually cause improvements?

Sequencing knowledge

When should each intervention occur?

Personalization knowledge

For whom does each intervention work?

Mechanistic knowledge

Why does the intervention work?

Environmental knowledge

What external conditions permit recovery to persist?

Economic knowledge

What does durable recovery actually save?

Implementation knowledge

Can an effective treatment architecture be reproduced?

These datasets would be useful even when an individual treatment failed.

A failed treatment arm would still identify a treatment sequence or patient subgroup that should not be prioritized.

In other words, negative findings would remain valuable.

