Long-Term Vision
The proposed institution should be conceived as a learning health system for addiction treatment.

Each cohort would contribute to improving the next cohort.

The system would progressively move from:

broad uncertainty

to

evidence-supported treatment pathways

to

adaptive treatment rules

to

personalized recovery trajectories

to

validated national treatment standards.

Eventually, the objective would be for a clinician to be able to answer something considerably more sophisticated than:

«"What treatment should this person receive?"»

Instead:

«"Given this person's characteristics, previous response, current psychological and biological state, social environment, and treatment history, what is the empirically supported next step in the recovery trajectory?"»

That is the level of precision toward which addiction medicine should ultimately aspire.

───

Conclusion
The central problem facing addiction treatment research may not be the absence of potentially effective interventions.

It may be the absence of an experimental system capable of determining how those interventions should be combined, sequenced, personalized, and embedded within a supportive environment over time.

Current research can tell us whether individual interventions sometimes work.

It is much less capable of determining how a complete recovery trajectory should be constructed.

The proposed National Longitudinal Addiction Treatment Research Center would address that problem directly.

By combining:

• a standardized residential environment;
• multiple ethically defensible treatment pathways;
• adaptive randomization;
• sequential treatment experiments;
• pharmacological and psychotherapeutic interventions;
• transitional housing;
• transportation;
• employment and education;
• long-term outpatient care;
• longitudinal follow-up;
• mechanistic measurement;
• and comprehensive economic evaluation,

the program could determine whether addiction treatment can be transformed from a collection of loosely connected services into an empirically optimized recovery system.

The immediate goal would be scientific knowledge.

The potential downstream benefits would be considerably larger:

more durable recovery, fewer overdoses, fewer hospitalizations, greater employment, greater housing stability, reduced criminal-justice involvement, improved quality of life, lower long-term healthcare expenditure, and potentially substantial net societal savings.

The proposal therefore represents not merely a new treatment center, but a new research architecture for studying recovery itself.

Its fundamental question would be simple:

«What is the most effective sequence of interventions and environments for producing durable recovery from substance-use disorder—and what happens when that sequence is delivered continuously, rigorously, and at sufficient intensity to give it a genuine chance to work?»

That question has not been adequately answered.

The proposed institution would be designed specifically to answer it